From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 17 Sep 2026 09:52:56 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] kosmickrisp: read a vertex attribute the pipeline does not
 describe

A vertex shader may declare an input that the pipeline's vertex-input
state leaves undescribed. Vulkan does not make that an error: the values
read are the implementation's default vertex attribute value, which
VK_KHR_maintenance9 publishes as
VkPhysicalDeviceMaintenance9PropertiesKHR::defaultVertexAttributeValue,
and this driver reports
VK_DEFAULT_VERTEX_ATTRIBUTE_VALUE_ZERO_ZERO_ZERO_ONE_KHR.

Neither fetch path could express such an attribute. The MSL backend
declares an [[attribute(n)]] for every location nir->info.inputs_read
names, while kk_gather_metal_vertex_fetch() fills the MTLVertexDescriptor
from the vertex-input state's attributes_valid, so an undescribed
location left Metal a [[stage_in]] member it had nothing to fill:
"Vertex attribute attribute_1(1) is missing from the vertex descriptor",
which kk_new_render_pipeline_state reports as
VK_ERROR_INVALID_SHADER_NV and which fails the whole pipeline, not just
that attribute. The lowered fetch is no better placed: its attribute
array holds PIPE_FORMAT_NONE there, which has no channel to decode and
no buffer to decode it from.

Resolve the read to the default value while the load still says whether
the shader wants a float or an integer, and take the attribute out of
the interface the two paths describe. x, y and z read zero and w reads
one, so a shader that declares a vec4 and a shader that reads only w
both see what the property promises, and Metal keeps filling the missing
components of a described attribute itself.

dEQP-VK.pipeline.monolithic.vertex_input.misc.unbound_input and
unbound_input_integer failed pipeline creation outright; both now pass.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
